4148 0 obj
  << /ActualText (The app\031s design was created with the help of  good old\031 pen \046 paper sketching\054 iterating with Claude AI on VS code\054 Figma and its Figma Make option\056 We wanted users to arrive on the app and be led through each step intuitively by the UI\056 \nA few challenges arose\072 \nHow do we make this app visually interesting when we are focusing on the most basic and simple interactions in workout tracking\077\nCan we somehow encourage commitment beyond sharing metrics recaps \077\n\nIn parallel\054 we also decided that my partner should take over the UI creation for the app\056 He is naturally visually creative and a gifted artist so the decision making process was very straightforward\072 he would go have fun creating designs and I would consult from a UX perspective\054 help us stay true to the vision we had for the app\056 \nAs a result\054 he came up with several designs until we landed on this one\056 \nThe mosaic background shows the user their big\055picture training results\072 each block represents their dedication and the time they spent adding to their life quality\056 It\031s not just a singular occurrence in a week or year\054 it\031s part of what they feed to their overall effort\056 Each workout is visually accounted for and as they amass\054 they create a visual that represents the bigger picture\056 Not just metrics or trends that exist only in the moment or on a report\056 \nMy partner drew inspiration from Lego for this\072 each piece just looks like a lonely piece on its own but stacked up\054 it creates a whole world\054 a composition made possible by each individual piece added\056 \n\n )
     /K [ 69 ]
     /P 4149 0 R
     /Pg 3146 0 R
     /S /Span
     /Type /StructElem
  >>
endobj

4149 0 obj
  << /K [ 4148 0 R ]
     /P 4166 0 R
     /S /P
     /Pg 3146 0 R
     /Type /StructElem
  >>
endobj

